 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The nearest-neighbor search is $O(N)$ per novel view, where $N$ is the number of training views. This is negligible compared to the rendering cost.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store training view positions.
-*   **Strengths:** Generalizes exposure correction to any viewpoint, not just training views [S1]. Simple to implement.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption of similar lighting between nearby views. May fail in scenes with rapid lighting changes or sparse training data [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When training data is captured with auto-exposure cameras and views are sufficiently dense [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When lighting conditions vary significantly over short distances or when training views are sparse [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The nearest-neighbor search is $O(N)$ per novel view, where $N$ is the number of training views [G]. This is negligible compared to the rendering cost [G].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store training view positions [G].
+*   **Strengths:** Generalizes exposure correction to any viewpoint, not just training views [S1]. Simple to implement [G].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption of similar lighting between nearby views [S1]. Fails when nearby views have significantly different exposure settings, as the nearest neighbor assumption breaks down and may cause the renderer to misinterpret exposure differences as high-frequency reflectance [S2].
+*   **When to use:** When training data is captured with auto-exposure cameras [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When lighting conditions vary significantly over short distances [G].
 
 ## Background
-The strategy was introduced in the context of Image-Based Gaussian Splatting (IBGS) to address photometric inconsistencies arising from auto-exposure in modern cameras [S1]. It builds on the observation that prior works [6, 17] only corrected exposure at training views, limiting their applicability to novel viewpoints [S1]. The related White Balance Initialization strategy was proposed in HDR-Plenoxels to resolve scale ambiguity between exposure and white balance, and between SH coefficients and white balance, ensuring stable optimization [S2].
+The strategy was introduced in the context of Image-Based Gaussian Splatting (IBGS) to address photometric inconsistencies arising from auto-exposure in modern cameras [S1]. It builds on [Radiometric Self-Calibration](1820-radiometric-self-calibration.md), which optimizes radiometric parameters during reconstruction, and addresses the observation that prior works [S1] only corrected exposure at training views, limiting their applicability to novel viewpoints [S1]. The related White Balance Initialization strategy was proposed in HDR-Plenoxels to resolve scale ambiguity between exposure and white balance, and between SH coefficients and white balance, ensuring stable optimization [S2].
 
